Appendix B — Full Proof of Theorem 1 (Monotone Mediation under Lossy Handoff)

Theorem 1. Let $\mathcal{I}_i$ be the information set available at arrangement $D_i$, and let $Q: \mathcal{I}_i \to \mathcal{I}_j$ be a Markov kernel representing the handoff to $D_j$. If detection success $D$ is a monotone function of the information set, and $Q$ is lossy (not injective on the support relevant to detection), then

P(D \mid \mathcal{I}_j) \;\le\; P(D \mid \mathcal{I}_i),

with strict inequality when $Q$ identifies states that are distinguishable for detection under $\mathcal{I}_i$.

Proof. The handoff forms a Markov chain $\mathcal{I}_i \to \mathcal{I}_j \to D$: detection depends on the earlier information only through the handed-off information. By the data-processing inequality for Markov chains, for any statistic $T$ and channel $Q$,

I(T; \mathcal{I}_j) \;\le\; I(T; \mathcal{I}_i),

where $I(\cdot;\cdot)$ denotes mutual information. The inequality states that no local operation on the signal (the kernel $Q$) can increase the information content about $T$.

If detection success is monotone in the available information—that is, if the detector's performance is a non-decreasing function of $I(T;\mathcal{I})$—then the probability of detection cannot increase when the information set is passed through $Q$. Formally, if $P(D \mid \mathcal{I}) = g(I(T;\mathcal{I}))$ for a non-decreasing $g$, then $I(T;\mathcal{I}_j) \le I(T;\mathcal{I}_i)$ implies $g(I(T;\mathcal{I}_j)) \le g(I(T;\mathcal{I}_i))$, which is the desired inequality.

Strict inequality obtains when $Q$ is non-injective on states that the detector can distinguish under $\mathcal{I}_i$: there exist $\iota, \iota' \in \mathcal{I}_i$ with $\iota \ne \iota'$ such that $Q(\iota) = Q(\iota')$ and $T(\iota) \ne T(\iota')$. In that case the channel collapses a distinction that the detector could have used, and the mutual information is strictly reduced, so $P(D \mid \mathcal{I}_j) < P(D \mid \mathcal{I}_i)$ whenever $g$ is strictly increasing on the relevant range. $\square$

Remark B.1 (The monotonicity assumption). The theorem is conditional on detection being monotone in information. This assumption can fail in pathological cases: a lossy summary might remove noise that was degrading detection, in which case $Q$ could improve detection despite reducing mutual information about the full state. The theorem therefore does not claim that lossy handoff always reduces detection; it claims that under the monotonicity assumption, the data-processing inequality transmits to detection probabilities. The assumption is stated explicitly so that it can be tested or refuted, rather than hidden in the interpretation of the result.

Remark B.2 (Relation to Fano's inequality). A complementary bound is available through Fano's inequality, which relates the probability of detection error to the mutual information between the state and the observation: $P(\text{error}) \ge 1 - (I(V;\widehat{V}) + \log 2)/\log|\mathcal{V}|$. Under lossy handoff, the mutual information in the observation is reduced, so the Fano lower bound on error is weakened upward. This gives a second, non-probabilistic route to the same qualitative conclusion: lossy mediation cannot improve the worst-case detection guarantee.

