Appendix D — Full Proof of Theorem 3 (Identification of Super-Additivity)

Theorem 3. Let $Y_j \in \{0,1\}$ be the gap status of arrangement $D_j$ on a pre-specified component, and let $E_1 = E_{ij}$ and $E_2 = E_{i'j}$ be two binary edge indicators with $i, i' < j$. Suppose:

1. Conditional exchangeability: $(Y_j(e_1,e_2))_{e_1,e_2 \in \{0,1\}} \perp (E_1,E_2) \mid X_j$.
2. Positivity: $0 < P(E_1=e_1, E_2=e_2 \mid X_j=x) < 1$ for all $(e_1,e_2)$ and all $x$ in the support.
3. Consistency: $Y_j = Y_j(E_1,E_2)$ when $(E_1,E_2) = (e_1,e_2)$.
4. No intermediate confounding: no variable affected by $E_1$ confounds the $E_2$–$Y_j$ relationship, and symmetrically.

Then the additive interaction contrast

\mathrm{RERI}(x) = p_{11}(x) - p_{10}(x) - p_{01}(x) + p_{00}(x),
\qquad
p_{e_1 e_2}(x) = P(Y_j(e_1,e_2)=1 \mid X_j=x),

is identified by the g-computation contrast

\widehat{\mathrm{RERI}}(x) =
\hat{P}(Y_j=1 \mid E_1=1,E_2=1,X_j=x)
- \hat{P}(Y_j=1 \mid E_1=1,E_2=0,X_j=x)

- \hat{P}(Y_j=1 \mid E_1=0,E_2=1,X_j=x)
+ \hat{P}(Y_j=1 \mid E_1=0,E_2=0,X_j=x).

Proof. Under conditions 1–3, each conditional risk $P(Y_j=1 \mid E_1=e_1, E_2=e_2, X_j=x)$ equals the corresponding counterfactual risk $p_{e_1 e_2}(x)$ by the backdoor adjustment formula applied to the graph in which $X_j$ blocks all backdoor paths from $(E_1,E_2)$ to $Y_j$. The conditional exchangeability assumption is the standard no-uncontrolled-confounding condition for the exposure–outcome relationship; positivity ensures that each conditioning event has nonzero probability in every stratum, so the conditional probabilities are well-defined; consistency connects the observed outcome to the potential outcome under the observed exposure. Condition 4 ensures that conditioning on $E_1$ does not open a collider path through a variable affected by $E_1$, which would otherwise invalidate the backdoor adjustment for the joint exposure. Substitution of the identified conditional risks into the definition of $\mathrm{RERI}(x)$ yields the g-computation contrast. $\square$

Remark D.1 (Interpretation of RERI). The relative excess risk due to interaction is the standard measure of additive interaction. $\mathrm{RERI} = 0$ means exact additivity; $\mathrm{RERI} > 0$ means positive (super-additive) interaction; $\mathrm{RERI} < 0$ means negative (sub-additive) interaction. On the risk-difference scale, $\mathrm{RERI} > 0$ is the criterion for super-additivity, and $\mathrm{RERI} < 0$ for sub-additivity. The accumulation conjecture is the claim $\mathrm{RERI}(x) > 0$ in at least one pre-specified stratum $x$.

Remark D.2 (Identification failure). Condition 1 fails when unmeasured institutional competence $U_j$ affects both edge formation and the gap. This is the default expectation in the delegation context, because competent institutions are both more likely to build mediation edges and less likely to exhibit gaps. Under failure of condition 1, $\widehat{\mathrm{RERI}}(x)$ is biased, and the direction of bias is not signed without further assumptions. The negative-control and sensitivity-analysis procedures of Appendix H are therefore mandatory, not optional.

