Appendix E — Logical Structure of the Reversibility Levels

The four reversibility levels are nested, not independent:

\text{Exercisability} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{Operational capacity} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{Legal authority} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{Existence}.

The contrapositive chain is:

\neg\text{Existence} \;\Longrightarrow\; \neg\text{Legal authority} \;\Longrightarrow\; \neg\text{Operational capacity} \;\Longrightarrow\; \neg\text{Exercisability}.

The conjunction of all four levels is therefore logically equivalent to the top level:

\text{Existence} \land \text{Legal authority} \land \text{Operational capacity} \land \text{Exercisability}
\;\equiv\;
\text{Exercisability}.

A delegation arrangement satisfies the reversibility condition iff the clause is exercisable. The lower levels are retained as failure diagnoses: a clause that fails at Level 1 is absent; at Level 2 is ultra vires; at Level 3 is operationally inert; at Level 4 is captured. The diagnostic value is in distinguishing the mode of failure, not in adding independent conditions to the satisfiability criterion.

